(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_zoology_principlesofinheritanceandvariation=[{id:"neet-2026-q137",pn:137,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Sickle cell anaemia",q:"The sixth mutant codon of beta globin gene causing polymerization of Haemoglobin and change in RBC shape is ____ .",o:["CAG","GUG","AUG","GAG"],c:1,s:`Sickle cell anaemia is an autosomal recessive disorder.
It is caused by substitution of glutamic acid by valine at the sixth position of the beta globin chain of haemoglobin.
This amino acid change results from a single base substitution, GAG to GUG.
Hence GUG is responsible for the change in the shape of RBC.`},{id:"neet-2026-q144",pn:144,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"ABO blood group inheritance",q:"What is the probability of having children with 'O' blood group, where both mother and father are heterozygous for 'A' and 'B' blood group, respectively?",o:["50%","0%","75%","25%"],c:3,s:`Parents are I\u1D2Ci and I\u1D2Ei (i recessive): I\u1D2Ci \xD7 I\u1D2Ei.
Gametes: I\u1D2C, i from one parent and I\u1D2E, i from the other.
Offspring: I\u1D2CI\u1D2E (AB), I\u1D2Ci (A), I\u1D2Ei (B) and ii (O).
Out of four possible children, one has blood group O.
So the probability of O blood group is 25%.`,sfig:{w:290,h:156,alt:"Cross I\u1D2Ci \xD7 I\u1D2Ei: gametes I\u1D2C, i and I\u1D2E, i give I\u1D2CI\u1D2E (AB), I\u1D2Ci (A), I\u1D2Ei (B), ii (O).",d:[["t",4,18,"Parent :","s"],["t",100,18,"I\u1D2Ci",""],["t",160,18,"\xD7",""],["t",200,18,"I\u1D2Ei",""],["l",160,24,160,40,"a"],["t",4,60,"gametes :","s"],["c",105,56,12,""],["t",105,60,"I\u1D2C","m"],["c",148,56,12,""],["t",148,60,"i","m"],["c",206,56,12,""],["t",206,60,"I\u1D2E","m"],["c",250,56,12,""],["t",250,60,"i","m"],["t",4,128,"F\u2081","s"],["t",105,128,"I\u1D2CI\u1D2E","m"],["t",150,128,"I\u1D2Ci","m"],["t",208,128,"I\u1D2Ei","m"],["t",252,128,"ii","m"],["l",105,68,105,114,""],["l",206,68,105,114,""],["l",105,68,150,114,""],["l",250,68,150,114,""],["l",148,68,208,114,""],["l",206,68,208,114,""],["l",148,68,252,114,""],["l",250,68,252,114,""],["t",105,146,"(AB)","ms"],["t",150,146,"(A)","ms"],["t",208,146,"(B)","ms"],["t",252,146,"(O)","ms"],["t",4,146,"Blood group :","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2026r-q145",pn:145,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"ABO blood group inheritance",q:"For a person with blood group \u2018O\u2019, which of the following is not a possible combination of parents\u2019 blood group genotypes?",o:["Father : IAi and Mother : IAi","Father : IBi and Mother : IBi","Father : IAIB and Mother : IAi","Father : IAi and Mother : IBi"],c:2,s:`When IA and IB are present together, both express their own type of sugars.
So an IAIB parent can never give a child of blood group O.
For a child to have type O blood, both parents must carry the recessive \u2018O\u2019 allele (i) and pass it down.`,sfig:{w:290,h:190,alt:"Cross: male I\u1D2CI\u1D2E \xD7 female I\u1D2Ci; lines from the gametes give I\u1D2CI\u1D2C (\u2018A\u2019 blood group), I\u1D2Ci (\u2018A\u2019 blood group), I\u1D2CI\u1D2E (\u2018AB\u2019 blood group) and I\u1D2Ei (\u2018B\u2019 blood group).",d:[["t",20,22,"\u2642","m"],["t",45,22,"I\u1D2C","m"],["t",80,22,"I\u1D2E","m"],["t",160,22,"\xD7","m"],["t",215,22,"I\u1D2C","m"],["t",245,22,"i","m"],["t",275,22,"\u2640","m"],["l",45,28,55,125,""],["l",45,28,120,125,""],["l",80,28,185,125,""],["l",80,28,250,125,""],["l",215,28,55,125,""],["l",215,28,185,125,""],["l",245,28,120,125,""],["l",245,28,250,125,""],["t",55,142,"I\u1D2CI\u1D2C","m"],["t",55,158,"(\u2018A\u2019 blood","ms"],["t",55,170,"group)","ms"],["t",120,142,"I\u1D2Ci","m"],["t",120,158,"(\u2018A\u2019 blood","ms"],["t",120,170,"group)","ms"],["t",185,142,"I\u1D2CI\u1D2E","m"],["t",185,158,"(\u2018AB\u2019","ms"],["t",185,170,"blood","ms"],["t",185,182,"group)","ms"],["t",250,142,"I\u1D2Ei","m"],["t",250,158,"(\u2018B\u2019 blood","ms"],["t",250,170,"group)","ms"]]}},{id:"neet-2026-q154",pn:154,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Sex determination XO type",q:"In a population of a grasshopper species, the chromosome number of some members is 23 and some other members possess 24 chromosomes. The 23 and 24 chromosome-bearing members in this species are ____ .",o:["all males","all females","females and males, respectively","males and females, respectively"],c:3,s:`Grasshopper sex determination is XX\u2013XO type.
Males have only one X chromosome besides the autosomes (XO). Females have a pair of X chromosomes besides the autosomes (XX).
So the individual with 23 chromosomes is a male and the one with 24 chromosomes is a female.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q173",pn:173,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Chromosomal disorders",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Down's syndrome is caused by the absence of one of the X-chromosomes.
Statement II: Turner's syndrome is caused by the presence of an additional copy of the chromosomes.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:0,s:`Down's syndrome is caused by an additional copy of chromosome 21 (trisomy 21). So Statement I is incorrect.
Turner's syndrome is caused by absence of one X-chromosome, i.e. 45 with XO. So Statement II is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2025-q145",pn:145,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Dihybrid cross",q:"Genes R and Y follow independent assortment. If RRYY produce round yellow seeds and rryy produce wrinkled green seeds, what will be the phenotypic ratio of the F2 generation?",o:["Phenotypic ratio - 1 : 2 : 1","Phenotypic ratio - 3 : 1","Phenotypic ratio - 9 : 3 : 3 : 1","Phenotypic ratio - 9 : 7"],c:2,s:`This is a classical dihybrid cross of Mendel.
A pure round yellow pea plant (RRYY) is crossed with a wrinkled green plant (rryy).
Yellow is dominant over green and round shape is dominant over wrinkled shape.
Phenotypic ratio in F2 is 9 : 3 : 3 : 1.
These are round yellow, round green, wrinkled yellow and wrinkled green.`,sfig:{w:260,h:90,alt:"The ratio 9 : 3 : 3 : 1 with arrows to round yellow, round green, wrinkled yellow and wrinkled green.",d:[["t",130,14,"9 : 3 : 3 : 1","m"],["l",110,20,40,48,"a"],["l",122,20,100,48,"a"],["l",138,20,162,48,"a"],["l",150,20,226,48,"a"],["t",35,62,"Round","ms"],["t",35,74,"yellow","ms"],["t",98,62,"Round","ms"],["t",98,74,"green","ms"],["t",162,62,"Wrinkled","ms"],["t",162,74,"yellow","ms"],["t",226,62,"Wrinkled","ms"],["t",226,74,"green","ms"]]}},{id:"neet-2025-q152",pn:152,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Pedigree analysis, sex-linked",q:"With the help of given pedigree, find out the probability for the birth of a child having no disease and being a carrier (has the disease mutation in one allele of the gene) in F\u2083 generation.",o:["1/4","1/2","1/8","Zero"],c:0,s:`In F\u2081 a carrier female and an unaffected (normal, not carrier) male had an affected male child, so the disorder is sex-linked recessive.
The consanguineous mating is between female (X\u1D9CX) and male (X\u1D9CY).
Punnett square gives X\u1D9CX\u1D9C, X\u1D9CY, X\u1D9CX, XY.
Out of 4 children only one is a carrier, i.e. 1/4.`,fig:{w:250,h:308,alt:"Pedigree of four generations F0 to F3 with a legend; the F3 children are marked with question marks.",d:[["r",99.7,12.3,18.7,18.7,""],["c",164,21.7,10,"F"],["r",36.7,56.7,18.7,18.7,""],["c",89,67.3,10,""],["c",89,67.3,3.5,"F"],["c",191.7,67.3,10,""],["c",191.7,67.3,3.5,"F"],["r",215.7,56.7,18.7,18.7,""],["c",47.3,115.7,10,""],["c",47.3,115.7,3.5,"F"],["c",94.3,115.7,10,""],["c",94.3,115.7,3.5,"F"],["r",154,104,18.7,18.7,"F"],["c",237.7,113.3,10,""],["r",53,155.7,18.7,18.7,""],["c",116.7,165,10,""],["c",168.3,165,10,""],["r",202.3,155.7,18.7,18.7,""],["t",62.3,169.7,"?","ms"],["t",116.7,169.7,"?","ms"],["t",168.3,169.7,"?","ms"],["t",211.7,169.7,"?","ms"],["l",119,21.7,154.3,21.7],["l",140,21.7,140,46.7],["l",89,46.7,191.7,46.7],["l",89,46.7,89,58.3],["l",191.7,46.7,191.7,58.3],["l",56,66,79,66],["l",66.7,66,66.7,93.3],["l",47.3,93.3,94.3,93.3],["l",47.3,93.3,47.3,105.7],["l",94.3,93.3,94.3,105.7],["l",201.7,67.3,215,67.3],["l",207.3,67.3,207.3,91.7],["l",163.3,91.7,237.7,91.7],["l",163.3,91.7,163.3,103.3],["l",237.7,91.7,237.7,103.3],["l",104.3,112,153.3,112],["l",104.3,115.3,153.3,115.3],["l",128.3,115.3,128.3,144.3],["l",60,144.3,210,144.3],["l",62.3,144.3,62.3,155.7],["l",116.7,144.3,116.7,155],["l",168.3,144.3,168.3,155],["l",211.7,144.3,211.7,155.7],["t",80,26.7,"F\u2080","s"],["t",12.7,70.7,"F\u2081","s"],["t",12.7,120,"F\u2082","s"],["t",12.7,169.3,"F\u2083","s"],["r",22.7,189,18.7,18.7,""],["t",49,202.3,"Unaffected male","s"],["r",22.7,213.7,18.7,18.7,"F"],["t",49,227,"Affected male","s"],["c",32,247.7,10,""],["c",32,247.7,3.5,"F"],["t",49,251.7,"Carrier female","s"],["c",32,272.3,10,""],["t",49,276.3,"Unaffected female","s"],["c",32,297,10,"F"],["t",49,301,"Affected female","s"]]},sfig:{w:250,h:110,alt:"Punnett square of female XcX with male XcY giving XcXc, XcY, XcX and XY.",d:[["l",100,10,100,100],["l",40,40,235,40],["l",48,16,98,40],["t",44,24,"\u2640","s"],["t",78,18,"\u2642","s"],["t",128,32,"X\u1D9C","m"],["t",196,32,"Y","m"],["t",62,66,"X\u1D9C","m"],["t",62,96,"X","m"],["t",128,66,"X\u1D9CX\u1D9C","m"],["t",196,66,"X\u1D9CY","m"],["t",128,96,"X\u1D9CX","m"],["t",196,96,"XY","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q185",pn:185,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Genetic disorders",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. Down's syndrome \u2014 I. 11th chromosome
B. \u03B1-Thalassemia \u2014 II. 'X' chromosome
C. \u03B2-Thalassemia \u2014 III. 21st chromosome
D. Klinefelter's syndrome \u2014 IV. 16th chromosome`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],c:0,s:`Down's syndrome is due to an extra copy of chromosome 21.
Klinefelter's syndrome is due to an extra X chromosome.
\u03B1-Thalassemia is controlled by two closely linked genes on chromosome 16 of each parent.
\u03B2-Thalassemia is controlled by a single gene HBB on chromosome 11 of each parent.
So A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.`},{id:"neet-2024-q197",pn:197,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"ABO blood groups",q:`As per ABO blood grouping system, the blood group of father is B\u207A, mother is A\u207A and child is O\u207A. Their respective genotype can be
A. I\u1D2Ei / I\u1D2Ci / ii
B. I\u1D2EI\u1D2E / I\u1D2CI\u1D2C / ii
C. I\u1D2CI\u1D2E / iI\u1D2C / I\u1D2Ei
D. I\u1D2Ci / I\u1D2Ei / I\u1D2Ci
E. iI\u1D2E / iI\u1D2C / I\u1D2CI\u1D2E
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["C & B only","D & E only","A only","B only"],c:2,s:`Father with blood group B\u207A has genotype I\u1D2Ei (or iI\u1D2E).
Mother with blood group A\u207A has genotype I\u1D2Ci (or iI\u1D2C).
Child with blood group O\u207A has genotype ii.
So only A is correct.`},{id:"neet-2023-q141",pn:141,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Klinefelter's syndrome",q:`Which of the following statements are correct about Klinefelter\u2019s Syndrome?
A. This disorder was first described by Langdon Down (1866).
B. Such an individual has overall masculine development. However, the feminine developement is also expressed.
C. The affected individual is short statured.
D. Physical, psychomotor and mental development is retarded.
E. Such individuals are sterile.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A and E only","A and B only","C and D only","B and E only"],c:3,s:`Klinefelter's syndrome is due to an extra X chromosome, karyotype 47, XXY.
The person is mainly male but also shows some feminine development, and is sterile.
So B and E are correct.
A, C and D describe Down's syndrome.`},{id:"neet-2023-q157",pn:157,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Chromosomal disorders",q:"Broad palm with single palm crease in visible to a person suffering from :",o:["Thalassemia","Down's syndrome","Turner's syndrome","Klinefelter's syndrome"],c:1,s:`Down's syndrome is caused by an extra copy of chromosome 21.
Signs: broad palm with a characteristic palm crease, short stature with a small round head, furrowed tongue and partly open mouth.`},{id:"neet-2023-q163",pn:163,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Pedigree analysis symbols",q:"Which one of the following symbols represents mating between relatives is human pedigree analysis ?",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:2,s:`Mating between relatives is called consanguineous mating.
It is shown by a double horizontal line joining the male square and the female circle.`,fig:{w:420,h:90,alt:"Pedigree symbols: (1) filled square, circle and diamond; (2) square and circle joined by a single line; (3) square and circle joined by a double line; (4) square and circle joined by a single line with a vertical line down to a filled square.",d:[["t",4,34,"(1)"],["r",24,22,16,16,"F"],["c",62,30,9,"F"],["p","M92 20L102 30L92 40L82 30Z","F"],["t",118,34,"(2)"],["r",142,22,16,16],["c",192,30,9],["l",158,30,183,30],["t",218,34,"(3)"],["r",242,22,16,16],["c",292,30,9],["l",258,28,283,28],["l",258,32,283,32],["t",318,34,"(4)"],["r",342,22,16,16],["c",392,30,9],["l",358,30,383,30],["l",370,30,370,62],["r",362,62,16,16,"F"]]},sfig:{w:200,h:50,alt:"Square and circle joined by a double horizontal line (consanguineous mating).",d:[["r",50,17,16,16],["c",112,25,9],["l",66,23,103,23],["l",66,27,103,27]]}},{id:"neet-2022-q103",pn:103,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Sex determination types",q:"XO type of sex determination can be found in :",o:["Birds","Grasshoppers","Monkeys","Drosophila"],c:1,s:`In XO type, males have only one X chromosome along with autosomes.
Females have two X chromosomes.
Grasshopper is the example.`},{id:"neet-2021-q184",pn:184,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Sickle cell anaemia",q:"In a cross between a male and female, both heterozygous for sickle cell anaemia gene, what percentage of the progeny will be diseased?",o:["100%","50%","75%","25%"],c:3,s:`Both parents are Aa (A normal, a sickle).
Aa \xD7 Aa gives AA : Aa : aa = 1 : 2 : 1.
Only aa is diseased, which is 1 in 4 = 25%.`},{id:"neet-2020-q014",pn:14,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"ABO blood groups",q:"Identify the wrong statement with reference to the gene 'I' that controls ABO blood groups.",o:["A person will have only two of the three alleles.","When IA and IB are present together they express same type of sugar.","Allele 'i' does not produce any sugar.","The gene (I) has three alleles."],c:1,s:`Gene I has three alleles IA, IB and i; a person has only two.
IA and IB together are codominant: each makes its own different sugar.
So the statement that they make the same sugar is wrong.`},{id:"neet-2020-q038",pn:38,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Mendelian disorders",q:"Select the correct match.",o:["Phenylketonuria \u2013 Autosomal dominant trait","Sickel cell anaemia \u2013 Autosomal recessive trait, chromosome-11","Thalassemia \u2013 X linked","Haemophilia \u2013 Y linked"],c:1,s:`Sickle cell anaemia is autosomal recessive, on chromosome 11.
Phenylketonuria is autosomal recessive, thalassemia autosomal and haemophilia X-linked.`},{id:"neet-2019-q037",pn:37,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Chromosomal disorders",q:"What is the genetic disorder in which an individual has an overall masculine development, gynaecomastia, and is sterile?",o:["Edward syndrome","Down\u2019s syndrome","Turner\u2019s syndrome","Klinefelter\u2019s syndrome"],c:3,s:`Klinefelter\u2019s syndrome is XXY.
The person is male-like but has breast development and is sterile.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2019-q079",pn:79,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Sex determination",q:"Select the incorrect statement.",o:["In domesticated fowls, sex of progeny depends on the type of sperm rather than egg.","Human males have one of their sex-chromosome much shorter than the other.","Male fruit fly is heterogametic","In male grasshoppers, 50% of sperms have no sex-chromosome."],c:0,s:`In birds the female is ZW and the male ZZ.
Sex of the chick depends on the egg (Z or W), not the sperm.
So statement 1 is incorrect.
The others are right: the human Y is short, male Drosophila is XY, and male grasshopper is XO so half the sperms have no sex chromosome.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`}]})();
